Yes, when conducting a process hazard analysis (PHA) using a Hazard and Operability Study (HAZOP) or any other approved methodology, the analysis must address both facility siting and human factors, rather than only equipment failures [1][2][3][5]. 

Regardless of the methodology selected, the PHA must address [1][2][3][5]:
- The hazards of the process [1][2][3][5];
- The identification of any previous incident that had a likely potential for catastrophic consequences in the workplace [1][2][3][5];
- Engineering and administrative controls applicable to the hazards and their interrelationships [1][2][3];
- Consequences of failure of engineering and administrative controls [1][2][3];
- Facility siting [1][2][3];
- Human factors [1][2][3]; and
- A qualitative evaluation of a range of possible safety and health effects of failure of controls on employees in the workplace [1][2][3].

Furthermore, a PHA focuses on equipment, instrumentation, utilities, human actions (routine and nonroutine), and external factors that might impact the process [4].
